.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-SET-INSTANCES 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-set-instances \- set a module's minimum and maximum instance count
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B emm set-instances
.BR \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
[\fB\-\-min\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-max\fR \fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the instance limits the manager's autoscaler works within for one
module.
.I \-\-min
is the floor kept running at all times,
.I \-\-max
the ceiling the pool may grow to under load. Either may be given on its
own; the one left out keeps whatever limit is currently in force, so a
ceiling can be raised without having to restate the floor.
.PP
The limits are persisted in the module repository and applied over what
.I euclid.json
configures, so a limit set here survives a manager restart and stays in
force until it is set again. They are held separately from the values the
manager writes back as instances start and stop, which are rewritten from
its own configuration on every instance state change and would otherwise
overwrite anything set here.
.PP
The change takes effect on the manager's next reconcile tick, within a
few seconds \- not at the moment the command returns:
.RS
.TP
.B Raising the minimum
starts instances immediately, until the pool reaches the new floor.
Without this the pool would sit below its minimum indefinitely, since
the autoscaler otherwise only grows a pool that is saturated, and an
idle one never is.
.TP
.B Lowering the maximum
starts nothing and stops nothing. The pool shrinks back below the new
ceiling through the ordinary idle scale-down (see
.IR euclid.manager.scale-down-idle-seconds ),
rather than an instance being stopped in the middle of a request to
satisfy a number that was just changed.
.RE
.PP
The caller must be authenticated as an administrator; see
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1)
for how that is checked.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
Module to set the limits for, e.g. \fIesm\fR. Required. Must be a module
the manager knows about \- an unknown name is rejected with
.I "HTTP 404"
rather than silently recorded.
.TP
.BR \-\-min " " \fIN\fR
Minimum number of instances kept running, 0 or more. Zero is allowed and
meaningful: it lets a module scale away entirely while it is idle.
.TP
.BR \-\-max " " \fIN\fR
Maximum number of instances the autoscaler may run, 1 or more.
.PP
At least one of
.B \-\-min
and
.B \-\-max
must be given. The two are checked against each other using whichever
limit is not being changed, so a minimum can never be left above a
maximum.
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I name
The module the limits were set for.
.TP
.I minInstances ", " maxInstances
The limits now in force \- including the one that was not given on this
call.
.TP
.I runningInstances
How many instances the pool holds at this moment. The manager moves this
toward the limits above on its next reconcile, so immediately after
raising a minimum it is still the old count.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The limits were accepted and recorded.
.TP
.B 1
The stored session is missing or not an administrator's, neither limit
was given, the module is unknown, the limits are crossed, the request
could not be sent, or the server rejected it. Details are printed to
standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Keep four ESM instances running at all times, and allow up to thirty-two
under load:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-instances \e
    --module esm --min 4 --max 32
.fi
.RE
.PP
Raise only EQS's ceiling, leaving its floor as it is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-instances \e
    --module eqs --max 16
.fi
.RE
.PP
Let ENS scale away completely while nothing is publishing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-instances \e
    --module ens --min 0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check what the pool actually looks like afterwards:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm list-modules
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
